A||||6||151 in. of love||advance?||||1||
Z||000000||151 in. of love||01-31-2001||05:43 PM||||I was wondering how the 2.5 duke <BR>advances or retards timing?<P>I saw an electrical diagram that says there is no mechanical or vacuum advance.||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||01-31-2001||06:35 PM||||You got it.  There is no (direct mechanical) vacuum, nor centrifugal spark advance.  The ECM does it.  <P>You <B>can</B> change the initial timing setting - but from that point, the computer (ECM) advances/retards the timing based on inputs from sensors.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||151 in. of love||01-31-2001||07:13 PM||||OK.<BR>How does the Computer do it?<BR>The distributer only goes around to the plug for so long.<BR>How can the computer advance or retard at all, if the distributer has already moved from the point of connection.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||01-31-2001||08:02 PM||||The distributor sends a reference signal to the ECM. The ECM does a bunch of calculations and sends the pulse to fire the plugs. If the distributor is to far off base timing the car won't run right or at all. The ECM can only acomodate so much error.<P>Base timing is used untill about 600 RPM. If it's to far off the car won't start. The ECM doesn't take over untill 600. below 600 the ignition module running off base timing is all there is.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tom Philips||02-01-2001||08:07 AM||||And just to add a little to theogre statement if you add 2 degrees of spark advance at base timing (say 12 BTDC) the ecm uses this extra 2 degrees through the whole range... so it is important to set base timing correctly and stay out of spark knock.... also for the people who disconnect egr the engine is prone to spark knock between 1300  plus  RPM as egr is used as a dillutant and the engine is more spark tollerant. This is all part of the ecm cal.||||reg||1
Z||000005||daved||02-01-2001||11:18 PM||||151", there is sufficient length in the distributor inside terminals (and the rotor tip) that they are in position to jump a spark for , say 15 deg of rotation, and since it's turning 1/4 slower than the engine, the ECM has lots of leaway to fire the ignition over a considerable range of crank angle.||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||02-01-2001||11:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by daved:<BR><B>...and since it's turning 1/4 slower than the engine...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Not to select sweaters (pick knits)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">, but for the record, the distributor turns 1/2 the speed of the crank, not 1/4.  <P>But anyway, who's counting? - daved is quite correct, the distributor has plenty of time to fire a given plug when it wants to.  But we'll not dwell on that...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
